            <ArticleTitle>[Abdominal pain without any identifiable cause: a guide for the general practitioner].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>456-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1024/0040-5930/a000193</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Abdominal pain without any identifiable cause represents a frequent phenomenon in clinical practice and a challenge for the physician. Prior to establishing the diagnosis of non-specific abdominal pain it is crucial to exclude a variety of potential causes. The differential diagnosis is long and includes functional problems but also extra-intestinal pathologies including thoracic, neurogenic, and metabolic causes as well as intoxications. Moreover, an intra-abdominal pathology, particularly a malignancy, must be ruled out systematically prior to labeling a patient as having &quot;non specific&quot; abdominal pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
